  "image_name": "2ee591e1ad67697d37bfcc2248625de8.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how mechanical strain changes charge distribution and band bending in piezoelectric semiconductors, both at metal contacts and at a p–n junction. Across the panels, stretching or compressing the piezoelectric region creates opposite bound charges at its ends, shown by red and blue regions with plus or minus signs. These piezocharges modify the local electrostatic potential relative to an unstrained case, indicated by colored energy-band curves compared with dashed reference curves.\n\nFor a metal–piezoelectric semiconductor contact, the sign of the applied strain determines whether the interfacial barrier is raised or lowered. One strain direction appears to accumulate negative charge near the metal interface, while the opposite direction produces positive charge there, shifting the band profile accordingly.\n\nThe lower panels extend this idea to a p-type / n-type (piezo) junction. Strain changes the depletion zone near the junction interface by introducing piezoelectric polarization charges on the n-type side. Depending on strain direction, the built-in potential and band bending are either enhanced or reduced, which would affect carrier transport across the junction. Overall, the image illustrates the core piezotronic mechanism: coupling mechanical deformation to electronic junction behavior through polarization-induced charges.",
